1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,627
This is the parang Ilang, the traditional
weapon of the Dayak people — the Iban,

2
00:00:06,667 --> 00:00:08,960
Kenyah and Kayan of Sarawak.

3
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:16,293
Since the old days this parang was used
to clear the forest, to hunt, to farm,

4
00:00:16,333 --> 00:00:20,000
to defend oneself, and for headhunting.

5
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:26,562
What makes the parang Ilang
different from an ordinary parang?

6
00:00:26,602 --> 00:00:29,544
Its blade is longer and stronger.

7
00:00:29,584 --> 00:00:36,000
It is forged from hard-wearing iron,
made for heavy work in the forest.

8
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,665
This parang has three parts.

9
00:00:39,705 --> 00:00:45,460
This one we make from iron, and
its carving we call kunding.

10
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:48,261
The sheath we make from wood.

11
00:00:48,301 --> 00:00:54,000
This is rattan, and this is
rattan too, with carving on it.

12
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:01,924
The process goes like this.

13
00:01:01,964 --> 00:01:05,124
First choose the material and heat the iron.

14
00:01:05,164 --> 00:01:08,833
Once the iron is red, we
forge and shape the blade.

15
00:01:08,873 --> 00:01:12,687
After that it is sharpened
and the surface decorated.

16
00:01:12,727 --> 00:01:16,000
Only then do we make the hilt and the sheath.

17
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:22,252
The final finish is rubbed with
sandpaper and coated with shellac.

18
00:01:22,292 --> 00:01:27,224
This sheath is made of selected
hardwood — meranti gerunggang,

19
00:01:27,264 --> 00:01:34,000
meranti empaling or rengas — decorated with
rattan weaving in the pusat bedus motif.

20
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:40,008
It is not decoration alone
— it strengthens the grip.

21
00:01:40,048 --> 00:01:45,055
Sometimes goat hide and hair
are added to the sheath.

22
00:01:45,095 --> 00:01:47,000
We call that gagung.

23
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:52,328
Every motif has its meaning.

24
00:01:52,368 --> 00:01:57,657
This sheath is carved with the
lemetak, remang and fish motifs.

25
00:01:57,697 --> 00:02:03,323
The blade is forged with motifs
inspired by the elements of nature.

26
00:02:03,363 --> 00:02:07,000
The hilt is carved with the hornbill motif.

27
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:14,987
In the past the parang Ilang was owned
by warriors and leaders of the community.

28
00:02:15,027 --> 00:02:19,960
It stood for courage, for skill,
and for the dignity of its owner.

29
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:27,440
It was also used in dance performances
and traditional ceremonies.

30
00:02:27,480 --> 00:02:33,333
Today it is more often a
heritage piece, a souvenir,

31
00:02:33,373 --> 00:02:37,000
and a symbol of Sarawak culture.

32
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:40,419
This is the parang Ilang.

33
00:02:40,459 --> 00:02:44,000
Every carving on it has its meaning.
